Apple is set to launch two new low-cost devices tomorrow, the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-17e/">iPhone 17e</a> and the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-neo/">MacBook Neo</a>. Both devices use A-series chips, which have historically been limited to the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/iphone/">iPhone</a> and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad/">iPad</a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/03/macbook-neo-edu.jpg" alt="" width="2005" height="1128" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1038577" /><br/>
The &zwnj;MacBook Neo&zwnj; has Apple's A18 Pro chip inside, which was first used in the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-16/">iPhone 16</a> Pro models, while the &zwnj;iPhone 17e&zwnj; has a newer A19 chip. Unsurprisingly, thanks to the newer chip, Apple's &#36;599 &zwnj;iPhone&zwnj; outperforms the CPU in its &#36;599 Mac.<br/>
<br/>
The &zwnj;iPhone 17e&zwnj; earned a <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/03/06/iphone-17e-benchmarks/">multi-core score of 9,241</a> on early Geekbench benchmarks, while the &zwnj;MacBook Neo&zwnj; <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/03/05/macbook-neo-first-benchmarks/">earned a multi-core score of 8,668</a>. Single-core chip results also favored the &zwnj;iPhone 17e&zwnj;, which earned a score of 3,607, while the Neo had a single-core score of 3,461.<br/>
<br/>
Metal scores for the GPU were closer, with the &zwnj;MacBook Neo&zwnj; <a href="https://browser.geekbench.com/search?k=v6_compute&amp;page=1&amp;q=mac17%2C5">scoring between</a> 30,000 and 31,400 the &zwnj;iPhone 17e&zwnj; <a href="https://browser.geekbench.com/search?k=v6_compute&amp;q=iPhone18%2C5&amp;utf8=✓">earned scores</a> ranging from 31,000 to 31,600.<br/>
<br/>
Both the &zwnj;iPhone 17e&zwnj; and the &zwnj;MacBook Neo&zwnj; have the same 8GB RAM for <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/apple-intelligence/">Apple Intelligence</a> support, and while that might not sound like enough for a Mac, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/review/macbook-neo/">early reviewers felt</a> that 8GB RAM was sufficient for everyday light workloads.<br/>
<br/>
